  "query_preview": "(Today through Friday)...\nIssued at 254 AM EDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nToday through tonight... \n\nA mid-upper level shortwave moving through the region today will \npromote increasing clouds. Modest forcing from the disturbance \ncombined with deeper moisture advecting into the area supports \nthe potential for scattered showers. The greatest coverage should \nbe across southwestern counties where the strongest forcing and \ndeeper moisture overlap. There remains some uncertainty regarding \nprecipitation chances further north and east due to lingering dry \nair in the lower levels, but isolated showers cannot be ruled out.\nLook for temperatures to remain cooler than normal due to \nextensive cloud cover and showers. Highs will generally be near \nthe upper 70s to near 80F. Highs may be slightly cooler than \ncurrently forecasted depending on cloud cover.\n\nChances for showers taper off late today into the evening hours once \nthe aforementioned wave shifts east. Lingering low-level moisture \nand light winds may promote fog development tonight. \n\nSunday through next week...\n\nModel guidance depicts an amplified upper ridge centered near the \nRockies with northwesterly flow across the Great Lakes Region during \nthe latter half of the period. This should allow for shortwaves to \npass through the region late this weekend into next week. These \ndisturbances along with deeper moisture advecting into central \nIndiana will return chances for convection to the forecast at times. \nWeak surface high pressure and an EML advecting towards the region \nwithin southwesterly should keep weather conditions mostly quiet \nthrough much of the day Sunday. \n\nBy Sunday night into Monday, most guidance suggest storm clusters or \nMCSs begin to develop within a very unstable environment across the \nGreat Lakes Region and propagating south or southeastward into the \nOhio Valley. Monday night currently looks to have the better chance \nfor impacts across central Indiana based on the current axis of the \nridge, but that looks to generally be more focused towards northeast \nIndiana. That being said, there may be a southwestward trend in \nwhere the storm complexes may impact as the global models often pin \nthe initiation point too far north and don't track the progression \nalong the theta-e gradient as well as some of the higher resolution \nmodels.\n\nDiverging model solutions leads to lower confidence regarding exact \ndetails during this period, but strong instability and enhancement \nof mid-upper level flow from the passing shortwaves could support \nthe threat for severe weather. High DCAPE values aided by steep \nlapse rates and the expected linear storm mode favors primarily a \ndamaging wind gust threat. PWAT values around 2.00 inches along \nwith warm rain processes may also result in localized flooding.  \n\nWarm air advection ahead of the aforementioned disturbances should \nwarm temperatures well into the 80s to lower 90s Sunday through \nTuesday. Dewpoints returning to the low-mid 70s will likely lead to \npeak heat indices around 100 to 105 degrees on Monday. Some \nuncertainty remains though as convection or convective cloud debris \ncould limit diurnal heating.\n\nFollowing the cold frontal passage early Tuesday, drier continental \nair will briefly build into the Great Lakes, pushing the primary \nthunderstorm boundary south toward the Ohio River. High temperatures \non Tuesday and Wednesday will hover in the upper 80s with reduced \ndewpoints. However, global ensembles agree on sub-tropical ridge \namplification over the Ohio Valley by Thursday and Friday. 500 MB \nheights rising above 590dm, combined with strong solar insolation \nand evapotranspiration, will drive maximum temperatures into the mid-\n90s with heat index values approaching triple digits by the end of \nthe period.",
